* START-HISTORY:
* 10 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 211: no locale variable set means UTF-8, as in $tk_open (a Windows console sets none of them).
* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 04f1eaa - the screen library as of S.75 TK-0.13 (containers and viewports, slider, selector, colour, accelerators, table styles)
* 12 Oct 26 dm  New.
* 19 Oct 26 dm  SL.S.DIFF (sl.h): text the terminal already shows in the same styles is not written again.
* 28 Oct 26 dm  S.88 phase 2 (S1): a control character in the text is never sent to the terminal (see the description).
* 28 Oct 26 dm  S.88 phase 2 (S1, review): the C1 controls too - bytes 128 to 159 on a terminal that is not in UTF-8 mode, C2 80 to C2 9F in UTF-8 mode.
* 3 Nov 26 dm  S.113 (owner: "All pages"): every write keeps the terminal's picture (SL.S.TERM, SL.S.TATTR); DIFF 1 compares against it and writes only the runs of
*              cells that differ; DIFF 2 changes the shadow alone (the next flush writes it).  The shadow is changed first, whatever is written.
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.186 (Fable's review; the owner: "the most reliable experience for the user"): on a UTF-8 terminal a whole character of two to four bytes is
*              written with blanks after it for the cells it does not fill (utf8.pad), so that it takes as many cells as the shadow gives it - one to a byte - and
*              columns stay in line and a write by difference stays true.  The text, the shadow and LEN() are unchanged.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_put(scr, x, y, text, attr)
*
*     scr    the screen context (sl.h).  Its SHADOW row (and its ATTRS row, if it has them) is changed
*            to match what is drawn, so scr must be the caller's own variable: it is changed in place.
*     x y    the cell of the first character, from 0 as in @(x, y)
*     text   one line.  A mark character (field mark and the like) is shown as a dot: the shadow is
*            a dynamic array.  A CONTROL CHARACTER is never sent to the terminal (28 Oct 26, S.88 phase 2, S1: text that
*            comes from data - a record, a record id, a lock id, a file name, an answer of the AI, a help page - can hold an
*            escape sequence, and sent raw it retitles the window, wipes the screen or types into the terminal): a tab is
*            shown as a blank, DEL as ?, and every other character below a blank as the letter that is 64 above it (ESC as [,
*            NUL as @, return as M) in the reverse style, ONE CELL for ONE CHARACTER, so the widths a caller worked out stay
*            right.  The shadow holds what is shown, not the control character.  THE C1 CONTROLS (28 Oct 26, review): 0x9B is CSI
*            on a terminal that is not in UTF-8 mode.  The terminal is taken as UTF-8 when LC_ALL, else LC_CTYPE, else LANG says
*            UTF-8 (UTF-8 or UTF8, as the IDE decides), or when the program opened the screen with LINES.  Not UTF-8: the bytes
*            128 to 159 are shown like the other controls, as the character 64 below (0x9B as [), reverse, one cell each.  UTF-8: the
*            two-byte form C2 80 to C2 9F is shown as a dot and that letter, reverse, two cells for the two bytes (the shadow counts
*            bytes); every other sequence, a curly quote E2 80 9C included, and the bytes 160 up outside UTF-8 mode, pass.  IN UTF-8 MODE A WHOLE
*            SEQUENCE IS FOLLOWED BY BLANKS (9 Nov 26): the bytes less the cells the terminal gives it (one, two for a wide East Asian character), so that
*            every character takes as many cells as it has bytes, as the shadow counts them.
*            What passes unchanged: every printable
*            ASCII character, the other bytes from 128 up (UTF-8 text, and the bytes of the glyphs a program writes itself) and the
*            nine stand-ins of $sl_line, CHAR(14) to CHAR(22), which this routine turns into line-drawing characters
*            below: they are the library's own glyph path, and are harmless as data (they become a line character).
*     attr   either one style code (n r u x, see $sl_attr) for all of text, or a string as long as
*            text with one code per character
*
*   Anything outside the screen, or outside SL.S.CLIP if the context has one, is not drawn and not
*   recorded.  The terminal is left in style n afterwards.  The cursor is left after the text, and
*   the context's own cursor (SL.S.CUR) is not touched: that is the program's, and the commands put
*   it back when they have finished.  SL.S.DIFF (sl.h) 1, on a screen whose terminal is known to match the library's picture of it:
*   only the runs of cells the terminal does not already show in these styles are written, and when there are none nothing is written
*   and the cursor is where it was.  SL.S.DIFF 2: only the shadow changes; the next flush ($sl_scr) writes it.  Every write keeps the
*   terminal's picture (SL.S.TERM, SL.S.TATTR) of a screen the library owns, so that what is compared is what the terminal shows.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_put(scr, x, y, text, attr)
$catalog $sl_put

$include sl.h

   common /sllin/ ln.on
   common /slctl/ ctl.from, ctl.to, loc.mode, c1.from, c1.to
   common /slu8/ u8.lead                                  ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.186): the lead bytes of a UTF-8 sequence

   * 28 Oct 26 (S1, C1 controls): is the terminal in UTF-8 mode?  Resolved once per process from LC_ALL, then LC_CTYPE, then LANG, as a shell does and as the IDE and $tk_open do (1 = UTF-8, 2 = not).
   *   A program that opened the screen with LINES has said so itself ($sl_line 'on'), and that counts as UTF-8 too.  A block of COMMON starts as 0 in every variable, or unassigned.
   if unassigned(loc.mode) then loc.mode = 0
   if loc.mode # 1 and loc.mode # 2 then
      loc = env('LC_ALL')
      if loc = '' then loc = env('LC_CTYPE')
      if loc = '' then loc = env('LANG')
      loc = upcase(loc)
      loc.mode = 2
      if index(loc, 'UTF-8', 1) > 0 or index(loc, 'UTF8', 1) > 0 then loc.mode = 1
      if loc = '' then loc.mode = 1     ;* 10 Nov 26 Windows port (RELEASE_1.1 211): no locale set means UTF-8 here, as in $tk_open
   end

   * 28 Oct 26 (S1): the control characters and what each is shown as; made once per process.  The stand-ins CHAR(14) to CHAR(22) are not in the list (the library's glyphs, below)
   *   and the tab is dealt with before it (a blank).  A block of COMMON starts as 0 in every variable, or unassigned.
   if unassigned(ctl.from) then ctl.from = ''
   if unassigned(ctl.to) then ctl.to = ''
   if len(ctl.from) < 20 then
      ctl.from = ''
      ctl.to = ''
      for ci = 0 to 31
         if ci # 9 and (ci < 14 or ci > 22) then
            ctl.from := char(ci)
            ctl.to := char(ci + 64)
         end
      next ci
      ctl.from := char(127)
      ctl.to := '?'
   end
   * The C1 controls, bytes 128 to 159 (0x9B is CSI on a terminal that is not in UTF-8 mode): each is shown as the character 64 below it (0x9B as [), as the C0 controls are 64 above
   if unassigned(c1.from) then c1.from = ''
   if unassigned(c1.to) then c1.to = ''
   if len(c1.from) < 32 then
      c1.from = ''
      c1.to = ''
      for ci = 128 to 159
         c1.from := char(ci)
         c1.to := char(ci - 64)
      next ci
   end

   * 9 Nov 26 (S.186): the lead bytes of a UTF-8 sequence (194 to 244), for utf8.pad's quick test; made once per process
   if unassigned(u8.lead) then u8.lead = ''
   if len(u8.lead) # 51 then
      u8.lead = ''
      for ci = 194 to 244
         u8.lead := char(ci)
      next ci
   end

   n = len(text)
   if n = 0 then return
   sw = scr<SL.S.W> + 0
   sh = scr<SL.S.H> + 0
   if y < 0 or y >= sh then return

   lc.all = char(14) : char(15) : char(16) : char(17) : char(18) : char(19) : char(20) : char(21) : char(22)

   * The style of every cell
   if len(attr) = 1 then
      cells = str(attr, n)
   end else
      cells = attr : str('n', n)
      cells = cells[1, n]
   end
   text = convert(@fm : @vm : @sm : @tm : char(255), '.....', text)

   * 28 Oct 26 (S1): a control character in the text is shown, not sent.  One cell for one character, so n and the cells are as they were; the character's cell is drawn in reverse.
   *   (COMPARE, not =, as below.)
   text = convert(char(9), ' ', text)
   safe = convert(ctl.from, ctl.to, text)
   * The C1 controls.  Not UTF-8: the bytes 128 to 159, one for one.  UTF-8: only the two-byte form C2 80 to C2 9F (a lead byte can only be C2 there; the bytes 128 to 191 inside a
   *   well-formed sequence, as in a curly quote E2 80 9C, are left alone), shown as '.' and the letter, two cells for the two bytes, as the shadow counts bytes.
   if loc.mode = 1 or ln.on = 1 then
      k = 1
      loop
         p = index(safe, char(194), k)
      while p > 0 do
         c2 = 0
         if p < n then c2 = seq(safe[p + 1, 1])
         if c2 >= 128 and c2 <= 159 then
            safe = safe[1, p - 1] : '.' : char(c2 - 64) : safe[p + 2, n]
         end else
            k += 1
         end
      repeat
   end else
      safe = convert(c1.from, c1.to, safe)
   end
   if compare(safe, text) # 0 then
      for ci = 1 to n
         if compare(safe[ci, 1], text[ci, 1]) # 0 then cells = cells[1, ci - 1] : 'r' : cells[ci + 1, n]
      next ci
      text = safe
   end

   * Clip to the screen and to the clip rectangle
   x0 = 0 ; x1 = sw - 1
   cl = scr<SL.S.CLIP>
   if cl # '' then
      if y < cl<1, 2> or y > cl<1, 4> then return
      if cl<1, 1> > x0 then x0 = cl<1, 1>
      if cl<1, 3> < x1 then x1 = cl<1, 3>
   end
   if x < x0 then
      cut = x0 - x
      text = text[cut + 1, n]
      cells = cells[cut + 1, n]
      x = x0
      n = len(text)
   end
   if x + n - 1 > x1 then
      n = x1 - x + 1
      text = text[1, n]
      cells = cells[1, n]
   end
   if n < 1 then return

   * The shadow: what the program has drawn, whatever reaches the terminal below
   row = scr<SL.S.SHADOW, y + 1>
   if len(row) < sw then row := space(sw - len(row))
   scr<SL.S.SHADOW, y + 1> = row[1, x] : text : row[x + n + 1, sw]
   ar = scr<SL.S.ATTRS, y + 1>
   if ar # '' then
      if len(ar) < sw then ar := str('n', sw - len(ar))
      scr<SL.S.ATTRS, y + 1> = ar[1, x] : cells : ar[x + n + 1, sw]
   end

   * S.113 (3 Nov 26): the terminal's own picture of this row (TERM, TATTR), kept on a screen the library owns
   has.term = (scr<SL.S.TATTR, y + 1> # '')
   dmode = scr<SL.S.DIFF> + 0
   if dmode = 2 then return                         ;* the screen is being drawn again for a flush that repaints or compares it: nothing is written now

   * 19 Oct 26: a form is drawing on a screen whose terminal is known to match (SL.S.DIFF 1): the same text in the same styles is already there,
   *   so it is not written again (the owner saw the whole screen flash each time a record or file was chosen: every widget was written again,
   *   the tree three times, where a handful of its rows had changed).  S.113 (3 Nov 26, owner: "All pages"): judged against the TERMINAL's
   *   picture, not the shadow, and cell by cell: only the runs of cells that differ are written, two runs being one when fewer than eight
   *   cells that agree lie between them (a cursor position costs about that).  A page of the toolkit that draws its whole body again for a
   *   cursor key writes the two rows that changed.  COMPARE, not =, which would take 007 for 7.0.
   runs = 1 : @sm : n
   if dmode = 1 and has.term then
      tr = scr<SL.S.TERM, y + 1>
      ta = scr<SL.S.TATTR, y + 1>
      if len(tr) >= x + n and len(ta) >= x + n then
         ot = tr[x + 1, n]
         oa = ta[x + 1, n]
         if compare(ot, text) = 0 and compare(oa, cells) = 0 then return
         gosub diff.runs
      end
   end

   * The terminal: for each run, the cell, then each run of one style
   out = ''
   for rk = 1 to dcount(runs, @vm)
      r0 = runs<1, rk, 1>
      r1 = runs<1, rk, 2>
      out := @(x + r0 - 1, y)
      gosub emit.run
   next rk
   display out :
   gosub keep.term                                    ;* MUTANT-POINT term
   return

* The runs of cells, r0 to r1 (from 1), whose text or style is not what the terminal shows (ot, oa); runs closer than eight cells are one
diff.runs:
   runs = ''
   di = 1
   loop while di <= n do
      if compare(ot[di, 1], text[di, 1]) = 0 and compare(oa[di, 1], cells[di, 1]) = 0 then
         di += 1
      end else
         dj = di
         dgap = 0
         dk = di + 1
         loop while dk <= n do
            if compare(ot[dk, 1], text[dk, 1]) = 0 and compare(oa[dk, 1], cells[dk, 1]) = 0 then
               dgap += 1
               if dgap >= 8 then exit
            end else
               dj = dk
               dgap = 0
            end
            dk += 1
         repeat
         runs<1, -1> = di : @sm : dj
         di = dj + 1
      end
   repeat
   return

* The terminal shows text and cells from x on now (the cells of them that were not written showed them already)
keep.term:
   if has.term then
      tr = scr<SL.S.TERM, y + 1>
      if len(tr) < sw then tr := space(sw - len(tr))
      scr<SL.S.TERM, y + 1> = tr[1, x] : text : tr[x + n + 1, sw]
      ta = scr<SL.S.TATTR, y + 1>
      if len(ta) < sw then ta := str('n', sw - len(ta))
      scr<SL.S.TATTR, y + 1> = ta[1, x] : cells : ta[x + n + 1, sw]
   end
   return

* One run, cells r0 to r1 of text: each part of one style, in its own style, and the style n after it
emit.run:
   last = 'n'
   i = r0
   loop while i <= r1 do
      c = cells[i, 1]
      j = i
      loop while j < r1 and cells[j + 1, 1] = c do j += 1 repeat
      call $sl_sgr(c, sq)
      seg = text[i, j - i + 1]
      if loc.mode = 1 or ln.on = 1 then gosub utf8.pad       ;* 9 Nov 26 (S.186): a character of several bytes keeps as many cells as it has bytes
      * the stand-ins of $sl_line become the bytes of a line-drawing character (or + - | if that is off)
      if convert(lc.all, str(' ', 9), seg) # seg then
         if ln.on = 1 then
            seg = change(seg, char(14), char(226) : char(148) : char(140))
            seg = change(seg, char(15), char(226) : char(148) : char(144))
            seg = change(seg, char(16), char(226) : char(148) : char(148))
            seg = change(seg, char(17), char(226) : char(148) : char(152))
            seg = change(seg, char(18), char(226) : char(148) : char(128))
            seg = change(seg, char(19), char(226) : char(148) : char(130))
            seg = change(seg, char(20), char(226) : char(150) : char(136))      ;* a full block
            seg = change(seg, char(21), char(226) : char(150) : char(178))      ;* a triangle up
            seg = change(seg, char(22), char(226) : char(150) : char(188))      ;* a triangle down
         end else
            seg = convert(lc.all, '++++-|#^v', seg)
         end
      end
      out := sq : seg
      last = c
      i = j + 1
   repeat
   if last # 'n' then
      call $sl_sgr('n', sq)
      out := sq
   end
   return

* 9 Nov 26 (S.186, Fable's review; the owner: "the most reliable experience for the user").  THE SHADOW COUNTS BYTES: every widget works out its widths with
*   LEN() and the library keeps one byte to a cell, so a character of two, three or four bytes that a UTF-8 terminal draws in ONE cell (two for the wide East
*   Asian ones) would pull the rest of the row to the left of where the library believes it is - columns out of line, and stale cells, since a write by
*   difference compares the shadow with what is not there.  So the terminal is made to match the shadow, not the other way round: a WHOLE, well-formed
*   sequence in seg is written followed by blanks for the cells it does not fill (bytes less its width).  A byte that is not part of one (a lone lead or
*   continuation byte, the bytes 248 to 255) is written as it is: the terminal shows it as one replacement cell, which is one cell for one byte already.  The
*   text, the shadow and LEN() are unchanged: SD still stores and compares bytes (the owner, 30 Oct 26).  Not UTF-8 mode: nothing to do, a byte is a cell.
utf8.pad:
   if convert(u8.lead, '', seg) = seg then return            ;* no lead byte of a sequence: nothing to pad
   u8.out = ''
   u8.n = len(seg)
   u8.i = 1
   loop while u8.i <= u8.n do
      u8.b = seq(seg[u8.i, 1])
      u8.k = 0
      begin case
         case u8.b >= 194 and u8.b <= 223 ; u8.k = 2 ; u8.cp = u8.b - 192
         case u8.b >= 224 and u8.b <= 239 ; u8.k = 3 ; u8.cp = u8.b - 224
         case u8.b >= 240 and u8.b <= 244 ; u8.k = 4 ; u8.cp = u8.b - 240
      end case
      if u8.k > 0 and u8.i + u8.k - 1 <= u8.n then
         for u8.j = 1 to u8.k - 1
            u8.c = seq(seg[u8.i + u8.j, 1])
            if u8.c < 128 or u8.c > 191 then
               u8.k = 0
               exit
            end
            u8.cp = u8.cp * 64 + (u8.c - 128)
         next u8.j
      end else
         u8.k = 0
      end
      if u8.k = 0 then
         u8.out := seg[u8.i, 1]
         u8.i += 1
      end else
         u8.w = 1
         begin case
            case u8.cp >= 4352 and u8.cp <= 4447 ; u8.w = 2          ;* Hangul Jamo
            case u8.cp >= 12909 and u8.cp <= 42191 and u8.cp # 12351 ; u8.w = 2      ;* CJK radicals to Yi
            case u8.cp >= 44032 and u8.cp <= 55203 ; u8.w = 2        ;* Hangul syllables
            case u8.cp >= 63744 and u8.cp <= 64255 ; u8.w = 2        ;* CJK compatibility ideographs
            case u8.cp >= 65040 and u8.cp <= 65049 ; u8.w = 2        ;* vertical forms
            case u8.cp >= 65072 and u8.cp <= 65103 ; u8.w = 2        ;* CJK compatibility forms
            case u8.cp >= 65280 and u8.cp <= 65376 ; u8.w = 2        ;* fullwidth forms
            case u8.cp >= 65504 and u8.cp <= 65510 ; u8.w = 2
            case u8.cp >= 127744 and u8.cp <= 129791 ; u8.w = 2      ;* pictographs and emoji
            case u8.cp >= 131072 and u8.cp <= 262141 ; u8.w = 2      ;* CJK extension planes
         end case
         u8.out := seg[u8.i, u8.k] : space(u8.k - u8.w)
         u8.i += u8.k
      end
   repeat
   seg = u8.out
   return
end

* END-CODE
